    <br />
    <hr size="1" />
    <div id="sC-component-footnotes" class="ts-footnote-2">
<p><a name="footnote1"></a><sup>1</sup> I would like to thank Linsey Molloy and  Kathryn Chen for their assistance in the preparation of these remarks, Darrian  Robinson and Anne Chen for their assistance with data, and colleagues in the  Federal Reserve System for insightful comments and suggestions. </p>
<p><a name="footnote2"></a><sup>2</sup> These  remarks are focused on the mechanics of policy implementation; see Williams, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2018/wil180928" target="_blank">&lsquo;Normal&rsquo;  Monetary Policy in Words and Deeds</a>, September 28, 2018, for a discussion of the  evolution of Federal Reserve policy actions and communications amid the exit  from the extraordinary policy accommodation, and his perspective on what  &ldquo;normal&rdquo; means. </p>
<p><a name="footnote3"></a><sup>3</sup> Also see Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2018/pot180803" target="_blank">Confidence  in the Implementation of U.S. Monetary Policy Normalization</a>, August  4, 2018, for a thorough discussion about the implementation of monetary policy  normalization.</p>
<p><a name="footnote4"></a><sup>4</sup> <a href="/markets/opolicy/operating_policy_170920" target="_blank">Statement  Regarding Reinvestment in Treasury Securities and Agency Mortgage-Backed  Securities</a>, September 20, 2017.</p>
 <p><a name="footnote5"></a><sup>5</sup> The effective federal funds rate (EFFR) is  calculated as a volume-weighted median of overnight federal funds transactions  reported by banks on the FR 2420 Report of Selected Money Market Rates. The New  York Fed publishes the EFFR for the prior business day, along with volumes and  rate percentiles, on its website daily at approximately 9:00 a.m. Eastern Time.  The EFFR is used as a measure of the typical federal funds transaction and is  the way the New York Fed&rsquo;s Open Market Trading Desk (the Desk) measures its  success in meeting the directive on interest rate control from the FOMC.</p>
  <p><a name="footnote6"></a><sup>6</sup> Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2016/pot160222" target="_blank">Money  Markets after Liftoff: Assessment to Date and the Road Ahead</a>,  February 22, 2016, provides a detailed discussion of the new framework. For a  discussion of the new and old frameworks, see <a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/jep.29.4.177" target="_blank">Ihrig, Meade,  Weinbach (2015)</a>. Logan, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2018/log180504" target="_blank">Operational  Perspectives on Monetary Policy Implementation: Panel Remarks on The Future of  the Central Bank Balance Sheet</a>, May 4, 2018, provides a comparison  of the technical operation of the pre-crisis and current frameworks.</p>
<p><a name="footnote7"></a><sup>7</sup> This one day was December 31, 2015, when the federal funds rate printed at 20  basis points, or five basis points below the bottom of the FOMC&rsquo;s target range.  Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2016/pot160222" target="_blank">Money  Markets after Liftoff: Assessment to Date and the Road Ahead</a>,  February 22, 2016, discusses this occasion.</p>
<p><a name="footnote8"></a><sup>8</sup> The  distribution of traded rates underlying the secured overnight funding rate  (SOFR) is broader than that underlying the EFFR and overnight bank funding rate  (OBFR). This reflects SOFR&rsquo;s construction, which was designed to improve repo  market transparency by including rates on transactions across three segments of  the repo market.</p>
<p><a name="footnote9"></a><sup>9</sup> I  discussed dispersion as a means to evaluate the efficacy of monetary control  and transmission in an earlier speech. See Potter, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2017/pot170405" target="_blank">Money  Markets at a Crossroads: Policy Implementation at a Time of Structural Change</a>,  April 5, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote10"></a><sup>10</sup> ON RRP take-up also remains well below early expectations: before lift-off,  median expectations for the level of ON RRP demand three years following  liftoff was around $250 billion.</p>
<p><a name="footnote11"></a><sup>11</sup> See Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2017/pot171011" target="_blank">Gradual  and Predictable: Reducing the Size of the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Balance Sheet</a>,  October 11, 2017, for a discussion of balance sheet normalization.</p>
<p><a name="footnote12"></a><sup>12</sup> <a href="/markets/opolicy/operating_policy_170920" target="_blank">Statement  Regarding Reinvestment in Treasury Securities and Agency Mortgage-Backed  Securities</a>, September 20, 2017.<p><a name="footnote13"></a><sup>13</sup> The dollar  values of securities held in the domestic SOMA portfolio refer to par values.</p>
<p><a name="footnote14"></a><sup>14</sup> The  Desk conducts small value purchases of agency MBS on a regular basis in order  to maintain operational readiness. Under these plans, the Desk may purchase up  to $300 million of agency MBS for monthly periods in which principal payments  fall below the cap and there is no reinvestment amount. These small value  purchases are concentrated in newly issued agency MBS in the To-Be-Announced  (TBA) market, which is expected to undergo changes next year due to the FHFA&rsquo;s  Single Security Initiative and the introduction of Uniform MBS (UMBS). Given  these changes, the Desk plans to develop its operational readiness for  conducting transactions in UMBS and, to more efficiently manage the SOMA  portfolio, convert some of its agency MBS holdings to UMBS where appropriate. See  the <a href="/markets/opolicy/operating_policy_180914" target="_blank">Statement  Regarding Agency Mortgage-Backed Securities</a>, September 14, 2018.</p>
<p><a name="footnote15"></a><sup>15</sup> In advance of each FOMC meeting, the Desk surveys <a href="/markets/primarydealers" target="_blank">primary  dealers</a> and active investment decision makers about economic and  financial market topics to gain insight into their expectations. See <a href="/markets/primarydealer_survey_questions.html" target="_blank">Survey  of Primary Dealers</a> and <a href="/markets/survey_market_participants" target="_blank">Survey of  Market Participants</a>.</p>
<p><a name="footnote166"></a><sup>16</sup> See the FOMC&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC_PolicyNormalization.pdf" target="_blank">Policy  Normalization Principles and Plans</a>, September 16, 2014.</p>
<p><a name="footnote17"></a><sup>17</sup> &ldquo;The Chairman suggested that the Committee  would likely resume a discussion of operating frameworks in the fall.&rdquo; See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20180801.htm" target="_blank">Minutes  of the Federal Open Market Committee</a>, July 31 – August 1, 2018.</p>
<p><a name="footnote18"></a><sup>18</sup> Assuming  that <strong><u>all</u></strong> banks place an equal  value on the next dollar of reserves, aggregate reserve scarcity is when that  value is above the rate at which reserves are remunerated, and aggregate excess  reserves is when that value is at or below the remuneration rate. </p>
<p><a name="footnote19"></a><sup>19</sup> The classic paper on this topic is <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/2325906?seq=1#metadata_info_tab_contents" target="_blank">Poole  (1968)</a>. A number of recent efforts have been undertaken to modernize  the Poole model by adding interest on reserves and other features of modern  money markets. Two notable examples of this are <a href="https://www.chicagofed.org/~/media/publications/working-papers/2018/wp2018-08-pdf.pdf" target="_blank">Schulhofer-Wohl  and Clouse (2018)</a> and <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/research/staff_reports/sr840" target="_blank">Afonso,  Armenter, Lester (2018)</a>. </p>
<p><a name="footnote20"></a><sup>20</sup> For a further discussion of the shape of the demand curve for reserves and its  implications for policy implementation, see Logan, <a href="https://www.newyorkfed.org/newsevents/speeches/2018/log180504" target="_blank">Operational  Perspectives on Monetary Policy Implementation: Panel Remarks on The Future of  the Central Bank Balance Sheet</a>, May 4, 2018.</p>
<p><a name="footnote21"></a><sup>21</sup> On  Oct 24, 2018, there was an increase in the share of federal funds transactions  trading above the IOR rate, reflecting an increase in bank borrowing from Federal  Home Loan Banks (FHLBs) to manage LCRs, rather than interbank lending.</p>
<p><a name="footnote22"></a><sup>22</sup> Alternatively, it could be that banks have become more efficient in their  balance sheet management, which would be consistent with the lower volatility  in overnight rates we have seen at recent quarter-ends. Bargaining dynamics  within overnight money markets, which exhibit a fairly high degree of  concentration of both borrowing and lending, could also have played a role in the  rise over time in market rates relative to the IOR rate. <a title="Chart" rel="facebox" href="#photo9">Figure 9</a> provides some  support for this interpretation. </p>
 <p><a name="footnote23"></a><sup>23</sup> Recent  market intelligence supports the notion that increased awareness among some  banks of the LCR benefit to borrowing federal funds from FHLBs, even at rates  at or slightly above IOR, has contributed to the upward pressure in federal  funds rates. For further discussion of the impact of LCR treatment on money  markets, see Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2018/pot180803" target="_blank">Confidence  in the Implementation of U.S. Monetary Policy Normalization</a>, August  4, 2018. </p>
 <p><a name="footnote24"></a><sup>24</sup> While trading volumes among smaller  domestic banks have been largely steady since the beginning of normalization,  their borrowing rates have increased since March with the rise in the broader constellation  of money market rates. This could suggest that the increase in funding rates  for these entities reflect bargaining dynamics, rather than increased demand or  aggregate reserve scarcity.</p>
<p><a name="footnote25"></a><sup>25</sup> See <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20150318.htm" target="_blank">Minutes  of the Federal Open Market Committee, March 17 – 18, 2015</a>. Recall that before liftoff, the  main concern was that rates would fall below the bottom of the target range.  Thus, policymakers thought term draining operations might be helpful.</p>
<p><a name="footnote26"></a><sup>26</sup> Analysis  of the data and extensive market intelligence suggested that the slight  increase in money market rates within the target range during the spring months  was primarily due to an increase in the supply of Treasury securities and an  associated rise in Treasury bill yields and repo rates. In particular, in the  first few months of the year, U.S. Treasury bill supply increased by over $300  billion, which contributed to a rise in dealer inventories likely financed in  the repo market. The resulting increase in bill rates and associated short-term  repo rates created an attractive alternative investment opportunity for federal  funds lenders, notably the FHLBs, which make up about 95 percent of current  overnight lending in the federal funds market. As federal funds borrowers  competed for a reduced pool of funds, rates rose. In addition, the gradual  decline in reserve balances due to balance sheet normalization, together with  changing dynamics within money markets—in part due to the regulatory  environment, which may have recently prompted some banks to pay up for federal  funds to improve their LCRs—may have helped push rates up a bit over time. For  a thorough discussion of this dynamic, see Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2018/pot180803" target="_blank">Confidence  in the Implementation of U.S. Monetary Policy Normalization</a>, August  4, 2018.</p>
 <p><a name="footnote27"></a><sup>27</sup> While the further move up in the EFFR relative to the IOR rate is not  surprising, the pace has been quicker than many market participants expected.  For example, in our July/August 2018 Desk surveys, only 33 percent of  respondents who provided expectations for this spread expected it to narrow to  two basis points or less by year end. On the other hand, the dynamics of the  decrease in the spread since June seem very similar to earlier periods, as can  be seen in <a title="Chart" rel="facebox" href="#photo9">Figure 9</a>. In particular, as noted above, rates on eurodollar transactions  have lagged.</p>
 <p><a name="footnote28"></a><sup>28</sup> The distribution of reserves throughout the banking system has important  implications for rates and volumes in the federal funds market. Federal funds  and other market rates above the IOR rate could reflect frictions related to  the distribution of reserves across the banking system; this dynamic may occur  at levels of reserves not consistent with aggregate reserve scarcity. For  further discussion, see Afonso, Armenter, and  Lester, <a href="https://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2018/07/size-is-not-all-distribution-of-bank-reserves-and-fed-funds-dynamics.html" target="_blank">Size  Is Not All: Distribution of Bank Reserves and Fed Funds Dynamics</a>, Federal Reserve Bank of New York <em>Liberty Street Economics</em> (blog), July 11, 2018.</p>
<p><a name="footnote29"></a><sup>29</sup> The  Federal Reserve is working on enhancements to the overnight bank funding rate  (OBFR) to capture wholesale borrowing activity, which moved from offshore to  onshore branches. Collection of these data began on October 1, 2018. There will  be a review of the new data and an evaluation of how its inclusion will impact  the OBFR before its expected addition.</p>
<p><a name="footnote30"></a><sup>30</sup> For more information about this survey, see <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/data/sfos/sfos-announcements.htm" target="_blank">Senior  Financial Officer Survey</a>, August 29, 2018.</p>
    </div>
         

</div>

</div>
